x86_64-linux  clang emits only the C2 variant of every constructor it writes for a class, where cxx1 emits C1 and C2 as it does for all of them. This is not about moves and not about this case: it is the same difference move-constructor.nonames records, and there it was reachable by defining constructors inside a class body. A constructor the *compiler* writes is inline by nature and has no out-of-line form to switch to, so every implicit constructor meets it - which is why this is the second case to, and no earlier one did: none both emitted an implicit constructor and compared its names. Measured with `struct Holder { Loud a; int n; };` and an implicit copy, which shows the same C2-only set with no move in sight. C1 and C2 are identical for a class with no virtual bases and both are emitted as mergeable definitions, so cxx1's extra C1 costs object size and nothing else. arm64-darwin emits both and agrees with cxx1 on all 39 names, and x86_64-windows agrees on all 20.
